\section*{Proof: Subset Precision is a Lower Bound}

Let $G_{true}$ be the total hidden ground truth of true matches, $G_{sub}$ be the trusted subset where $G_{sub} \subseteq G_{true}$, and $M$ be the set of matches predicted by the model.

\subsection*{Definitions}

The true precision and the subset precision are defined as:
\[
\text{Precision}_{true} = \frac{|M \cap G_{true}|}{|M|}
\]
\[
\text{Precision}_{sub} = \frac{|M \cap G_{sub}|}{|M|}
\]

\subsection*{Inequality Proof}

Since the trusted subset is a subset of the total true matches:
\[
G_{sub} \subseteq G_{true}
\]

By the monotonicity of set intersections, intersecting both sides with the model predictions $M$ yields:
\[
(M \cap G_{sub}) \subseteq (M \cap G_{true})
\]

Taking the cardinality (size) of both sets preserves the inequality:
\[

|M \cap G_{sub}| \leq |M \cap G_{true}|
\]

Dividing both sides by the strictly positive total number of model predictions $|M|$ gives:
\[
\frac{|M \cap G_{sub}|}{|M|} \leq \frac{|M \cap G_{true}|}{|M|}
\]

Substituting the original definitions completes the proof:
\[
\text{Precision}_{sub} \leq \text{Precision}_{true} \quad \blacksquare
\]

