Let $\mathcal{M}$ be the global set of true matches, $\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}$ be the exact artist match subset, and $\mathcal{A}$ be the model predictions. We isolate two distinct fractional components within our evaluation framework:

\begin{equation}
p = \frac{|\mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|} \quad \text{(The True Match Prior / Prevalence Proportion)}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
q = \frac{|\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|}{|\mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|} \quad \text{(The Subset Capturing Success Rate / Estimated Recall } \widehat{R}_{\text{eval}}\text{)}
\end{equation}

\vspace{2em}

\subsection*{Argument 1: Preservation of the True Match Prior ($p$)}

Because the artificial noising process on the artist column is uniform and random, the clean-artist property $\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}$ is statistically independent of the true latent matching state $\mathcal{M}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}} \perp \!\! \perp \mathcal{M} \implies \frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}} \cap \mathcal{M}|}{|\mathcal{M}|} = \frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|}{|\mathcal{P}|}
\end{equation}

By re-arranging the terms, we formally prove that your baseline proportion $p$ matches global reality:
\begin{equation}
p = \frac{|\mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|} = \frac{|\mathcal{M}|}{|\mathcal{P}|} = p_{\text{global}}
\end{equation}

\vspace{2em}

\subsection*{Argument 2: Clustered Noise and Captured Predictive Inflation ($q$)}

Let $\mathcal{Q} \in \{0, 1\}$ be a latent data quality indicator, where $\mathcal{Q} = 1$ represents uncorrupted records. 

In real-world data streams, data quality naturally travels in systemic clusters across columns:
\begin{equation}
\text{Quality(Song Features)} \not\perp \!\! \perp \text{Quality(Artist Name)}
\end{equation}

Therefore, pulling record pairs into $\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}$ drastically forces the latent quality state $\mathcal{Q} \to 1$:
\begin{equation}
\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|} \gg \frac{|\{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|}{|\mathcal{P}|}
\end{equation}

Let represent the song features used by your model. The model's prediction success depends on $\mathcal{Q}$:
\begin{equation}
\left( \frac{|\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{M} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|}{|\mathcal{M} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|} \right) \gg \left( \frac{|\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{M} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=0\}|}{|\mathcal{M} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=0\}|} \right)
\end{equation}

Expanding your subset success rate $q$ over the hidden data quality distribution reveals the inflation:
\begin{equation}
q = \underbrace{\left( \frac{|\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|}{|\mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|} \right)}_{\text{Artificially maximized success rate}} \cdot \left( \frac{|\mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}} \cap \{\mathcal{Q}=1\}|}{|\mathcal{M} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{clean}}|} \right) + \epsilon
\end{equation}

Because the weight of the pristine $\{\mathcal{Q}=1\}$ records is highly concentrated inside your evaluation set:
\begin{equation}
q \gg \frac{|\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{M}|}{|\mathcal{M}|} = R_{\text{global}}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Conclusion 2:} The model captures a significantly higher proportion of positives inside this subset than globally.

